======================== Isolation Problems ========================

Dirty read:
  Read uncommitted data of another transaction 
  T1: update account set balance = 50
  T2: reads 50 
  T1: Rollback 
  Now balance is actually 100 but T2 reads 50

Non repeatable read:
  Same row reads twice -> different value
  T1: select balance -> 100
  T2: update balance = 150; commit
  T1: select balance -> 150

Phantom read:
  New rows appears of disappears between queries 
  T1: select * from orders where amount > 100 (returns 5 rows)
  T2: insert order(amount=200)
  T1: select again -> returns 6 rows

Lost update:
  Balance is 100
  T1: reads 100
  T2: reads 100

  T1 updates -> 80
  T2 updates -> 70 (overwrites T1)

======================== Database Indexes ========================

Indexes are data structures that help databases find rows faster without scanning the full table.

Internally, indexes map column values to row locations.

Example:
a@gmail.com -> row_id 1523

Most databases use:
B-Tree indexes
for efficient searching and sorting.

======================== Composite Index ========================

INDEX(user_id, created_at)
Leftmost Prefix Rule

Works for:
user_id
user_id + created_at

Does NOT work efficiently for:
created_at only

because indexes are ordered from left to right.

======================== Why Indexes Slow Down Writes ========================

Indexes improve reads but increase write cost.

When inserting/updating rows:
table data updates
all related indexes also update

Example:
INSERT row
-> update table
-> update indexes

More indexes:
slower writes
higher storage usage
more memory consumption

======================== When Indexes Are Not Used ========================
1. Low Cardinality Columns

Columns with very few unique values are poor candidates for indexing.

Example:
gender:
M -> 60%
F -> 40%
DB may prefer full table scan because index selectivity is poor.

2. Using Functions on Indexed Columns
Example:
WHERE LOWER(email) = 'a@gmail.com'

Applying functions can prevent normal index usage because indexed values are transformed during query execution.

3. Leading Wildcards
LIKE '%abc%'

B-Tree indexes cannot efficiently search from the middle of a string.

Works better:
LIKE 'abc%'
because index traversal can start from prefix order.

