======================== Physical Storage of Index ========================

Indexes are stored separately from table data.

In most relational databases:

Table rows -> Data Pages
Indexes -> Index Pages

Stored on:

SSD/HDD
frequently accessed parts cached in RAM

======================== How DB Uses an Index ========================

Query execution flow:

Check if matching index exists
Traverse B-Tree
Root -> Internal Nodes -> Leaf Node
Find row pointer
Fetch actual row from table

======================== Normalization ========================
1NF
Atomic/single values only.

2NF
No partial dependency on composite key.

3NF
No transitive dependency.

Over Normalization
Too many joins 
Slow read performance
Complex queries 

======================== How DB Executes a JOIN ========================
1. Nested Loop Join
For each row in table A, DB scans matching rows in table B.
Slow without indexes.

2. Index Nested Loop Join (Most Common)
For each row in table A:
Use index on table B
Very efficient when join columns are indexed.

======================== Best Practices for JOIN ========================
Index foreign keys
Avoid SELECT *
Join smaller datasets first

======================== JOIN vs Denormalization ========================

Many joins:
Normalized -> slower reads

Denormalization:
Duplicated data -> faster reads

Real systems usually:
Normalize core data
Denormalize hot/read-heavy paths

Example:
Movies + Theatres + Shows + Seats

Heavy joins can be optimized using:
cached responses
pre-joined views
read models

