======================== How Replication Works ========================

Flow:
Primary -> WAL/Binlog -> Replica
Write on primary
Change written to WAL/Binlog
Replica reads logs
Replica replays changes

======================== Replication Modes ========================
Asynchronous
Primary does not wait for replica.
✅ Fast writes
❌ Possible lag

Semi-Synchronous
Waits for at least one replica ACK.
❌ Slower writes
✅ Better durability

Synchronous
Waits for all replicas.
✅ Strong consistency
❌ High latency

======================== Sharding ========================

Sharding splits data across multiple databases to distribute load and storage.
Types of Sharding
1. Range Based
1-1000 -> Shard A
1001-2000 -> Shard B

2. Hash Based
user_id % 4
Distributes data more evenly.

3. Directory Based
A lookup service decides which shard stores the data.

Shard Key
Column used to decide where data lives.
user_id
tenant_id
region

Query With Shard Key
Efficient:
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = 10
DB directly knows target shard.

Query Without Shard Key
Expensive:
Search all shards
called:
Scatter-Gather Query

======================== Hot Shard Problem ========================

One shard receives disproportionately high traffic.
Celebrity user -> all traffic hits one shard

Problems:
uneven load
CPU bottleneck
storage imbalance
Fixes
better shard key
hashing
resharding
consistent hashing

======================== SQL Horizontal Partitioning Tradeoffs ========================
1. Joins Become Hard
Cross-shard joins are expensive.
Query Shard A
Query Shard B
Move data across network
Merge results

This becomes:
Distributed Join
not a normal local join anymore.

2. Transactions Become Complex
ACID across shards is difficult.
Requires:
2PC (Two-Phase Commit)
sagas
distributed coordination

3. Operational Complexity
resharding requires data movement
shard mapping management
routing logic in application

4. Limited Flexibility
Changing shard key later is extremely painful.

