======================== Why NoSQL Partitioning is Different from SQL Sharding ========================
SQL Sharding

In SQL systems:

each shard is a separate database
application decides shard routing

Example:

Shard1 -> users 1-250k
Shard2 -> users 250k-500k

Problems:

app must know shard logic
adding new DBs is painful
rebalancing requires app changes
shard management becomes operationally complex
NoSQL Partitioning

In NoSQL:

key -> hash(key) -> partition -> node

Application simply does:

get(userId)

Database automatically handles:

partition selection
node routing
replication
failover

App does not know where data lives.

======================== Why Partitions Exist ========================

Why not directly map data to nodes?

Because:

Nodes change frequently
Data distribution should stay stable

Nodes:

fail
get added/removed
become slow

Partitions sit between:

Data <-> Nodes

This allows independent:

movement
balancing
replication

======================== Problems Partitions Solve ========================
1. Easier Rebalancing
Without partitions:
Node A -> 50%
Node B -> 50%

Add Node C:
Move huge amount of data manually

With partitions:
100 partitions total
Only some partitions move to new node.
Much cheaper rebalancing.

2. Hot Partition Handling
Example:
user:1 -> millions of requests

Partitioning allows DB to:
isolate hot data
rebalance independently
move partitions separately

3. Failure Recovery
If:
Node B dies
DB can:
promote replica partitions
reassign ownership
recover automatically

Without partitions, entire shard handling becomes harder.

======================== Suitcase Analogy ========================

Without partitions:
Node = suitcase
Data = clothes

Adding new suitcase:
Move clothes manually

With partitions:
Partitions = small bags
Nodes = suitcases

Adding new node:
Move only few bags

======================== Why SQL Cannot Easily Use Consistent Hashing ========================

SQL databases are built around:
strong ACID transactions
joins
foreign keys
local consistency

These assume:
Data and locks are local

Consistent hashing assumes:
data can live anywhere
nodes can fail anytime
network partitions happen

These ideas conflict with traditional SQL guarantees.
That is why:

NoSQL prioritizes scalability/distribution
SQL prioritizes consistency/transactions